UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 22, Lesson 1: Character traits and evidence', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Character traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nName a trait and evidence for: \"Lina returned the lost purse before checking the shops.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nTrait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first.\n\nCHECK\nListing appearance alone does not prove a character trait.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFor two characters, record three traits each with quoted or paraphrased evidence from actions or dialogue. Explain one trait that changes after an event.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Character traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: For two characters, record three traits each with quoted or paraphrased evidence from actions or dialogue. Explain one trait that changes after an event. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first. Reasoning: I used trait by attending to evidence and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. Correction: Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first. Why: the correction restores accurate use of trait.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating trait on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with trait, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first. This matches the lesson skill for trait.","options":["Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first.","Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Name a trait and evidence for: \"Lina returned the lost purse before checking the shops.\" Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first.","Careful use of evidence and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait.","Character traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising trait?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named evidence and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the evidence and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising trait. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries trait.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show trait and say what they do."],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses trait from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first.","Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about trait?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise evidence and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about trait.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of trait to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating trait on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first. Reasoning: I used trait by attending to evidence and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with trait, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. Correction: Trait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first. Why: the correction restores accurate use of trait.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w22-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise trait.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use trait with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify evidence and explain why it matters.","I can practise action independently on a new prompt.","I can notice dialogue needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Character traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Character traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trait?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWhen Sam shares the last apple without being asked, readers can infer kindness. Evidence: the sharing action, not only the word kind in narration.\n\nTRY FIRST\nName a trait and evidence for: \"Lina returned the lost purse before checking the shops.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nTrait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first.","explain":"Character traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Trait inferred from a generous action.","caption":"Base traits on evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Action evidence</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">shares last apple</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Trait</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">kindness</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: When Sam shares the last apple without being asked, readers can infer kindness. Evidence: the sharing action, not only t Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: When Sam shares the last apple without being asked, readers can infer kindness. Evidence: the sharing action, not only t Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trait?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWhen Sam shares the last apple without being asked, readers can infer kindness. Evidence: the sharing action, not only the word kind in narration. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Honest action contrasted with appearance-only notes.","caption":"Appearance alone rarely proves a trait.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Dialogue/action</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">returned purse first</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not enough</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">hair colour only</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trait?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nListing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. Here is a more productive model: When Sam shares the last apple without being asked, readers can infer kindness. Evidence: the sharing action, not only the word kind in narration.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nTrait: honest/responsible. Evidence: returned the purse first.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Listing appearance alone does not prove a character trait. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trait?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCharacter traits are qualities we infer from actions, dialogue and reactions, always backed by evidence. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 22, Lesson 1: Character traits and evidence","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Class novel study","week":22,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nWhen Sam shares the last apple without being asked, readers can infer kindness. Evidence: the sharing action, not only the word kind in narration.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Trait inferred from a generous action.","caption":"Base traits on evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Action evidence</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">shares last apple</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Trait</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">kindness</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Honest action contrasted with appearance-only notes.","caption":"Appearance alone rarely proves a trait.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\